  "grammar_explanations": [
    {
      "slug": "present simple: affirmative with feel/have",
      "title_form": "Presente simple: afirmativo con feel y have",
      "title_target": "Present simple: affirmative with feel and have",
      "description_md_form": "Para describir cómo te sientes, usamos el **presente simple** con los verbos **feel** (sentirse) y **have** (tener).\n\n**Con feel:**\n- **I feel** sick. = Me siento mal.\n- **She feels** tired. = Ella se siente cansada.\n\n**Con have:**\n- **I have** a headache. = Tengo dolor de cabeza.\n- **He has** a fever. = Él tiene fiebre.\n\n**Regla clave:** con *he*, *she* e *it*, agregamos **-s** al verbo: *feel* → *feels*, *have* → *has*.\n\n**Estructura:**\n- Sujeto + **feel/feels** + adjetivo (sick, tired, ill).\n- Sujeto + **have/has** + sustantivo (headache, fever, flu, cough, pain).\n\nUsamos estas frases para hablar de síntomas comunes: *I have a sore throat*, *She has the flu*, *We feel tired*.",
      "description_md_target": "To describe how you feel, we use the **present simple** with the verbs **feel** and **have**.\n\n**With feel:**\n- **I feel** sick.\n- **She feels** tired.\n\n**With have:**\n- **I have** a headache.\n- **He has** a fever.\n\n**Key rule:** with *he*, *she* and *it*, we add **-s** to the verb: *feel* → *feels*, *have* → *has*.\n\n**Structure:**\n- Subject + **feel/feels** + adjective (sick, tired, ill).\n- Subject + **have/has** + noun (headache, fever, flu, cough, pain).\n\nWe use these phrases to talk about common symptoms: *I have a sore throat*, *She has the flu*, *We feel tired*.",
      "grammar_structure": "Subject + feel/feels + adjective | Subject + have/has + noun",
